{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1522715-scarlet-empress-personality-inspiration-and-a-possible-story-plan","id":1522715,"title":"Scarlet Empress Personality Inspiration and a possible story plan","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-09-17T11:26:24-07:00","id":1522715,"title":"Scarlet Empress Personality Inspiration and a possible story plan","content":"For the most part, the Scarlet Empress's personality is largely left blank for the ST to decide on what fits best for their campaign with one notable exception where she's duped by the Ebon Dragon and is a major villain. I've never really liked that storyline and I wanted to add an alternate take so that storytellers can have multiple reference points. What I recommend is taking inspiration from the Phoenix Empress from the Vlad Taltos books by Steven Brust.<br /> Brust is mentioned as being an inspiration from Exalted but damn is a lot of the story and flavor directly lifted from his novels. Just sticking to the Phoenix Empress versus the Scarlet Empress, during an interregnum, while disease is rampant and part of the world has been dissolved into the primordial chaos, the gods are worried that the jenoine, who are sealed from the world and used to have several of the gods as slaves and basically stand in for the yozi, are going to be soon making a move. So, a previously unknown young woman and her cadre of friends make their way to the sealed up area where no one can survive going to retrieve the powerful artifact to protect the world. A previously not very important city becomes her new capital from which she then unites the houses of dragaerans (dragon-blood) under her except for areas that are geographically hard to get to, such as the islands to the West and the Eastern half of her continent. While she does this, she's advised by her Prime Minister who's a spy master that most people don't know exist.<br /> Now, some of these elements are common to fantasy, so of course there will be overlap, but there are too many to ignore from geography to the fact that it's the Phoenix Empress and the Scarlet Empress's symbol is a garda bird (a phoenix) despite the fact that people have pointed out she's most likely an Earth aspect.<br /> If you do want to utilize her as a reference point, especially for why and how she disappeared, then here's a list of aspects:<ul><li>The Phoenix Empress is negotiating the ins and outs of the political empire and is aware that every cog connects with another and if she acts openly or rashly a lot of pieces fall apart.</li> <li>The Phoenix Empress doesn't like the inequality and problems that exist in her realm but always compares things to the horrors of the past and her own internal concerns of slipping and becoming a tyrant.</li> <li>She plays at being aloof and cold in public, but in private she's much more thoughtful and quiet, as she's always trying to understand what the right thing is.</li> <li>To accomplish things that she can't openly support, she'll utilize a trusted few, or secretly manipulate events for some wild cards to do things that she can then publicly denounce, but then do nothing about stopping.</li> <li>She's very lonely and only has a few people she trusts. She also knows that she's being increasingly targeted and embroiled in more and more schemes and is increasingly worried that she's become the linchpin so if something happens to her, especially if she becomes more awful, than it becomes a problem for the empire.</li> <li>In the books, we know that in the future she disappears. The woman who's supposed to be her successor takes over, and this then ushers a new age where things are more enlightened. The Phoenix Empress is in the background with her human love finally able to enjoy her life rather than being a symbol.</li> </ul>To this end, here's an alternate version of how you could run the missing Scarlet Empress storyline:<ul><li>The Scarlet Empress has chosen the Roseblack to be her successor even though she's not directly in line.</li> <li>She knows the truth of the Immaculate Faith and the rise of the Solar Exalted isn't perceived as a threat per se, but an opportunity.</li> <li>She's been dealing with people manipulating her since the beginning, whether it be Sidereals or Yozi, and is trying to extricate herself.</li> <li>If you start the storyline off before the Empress's disappearance, you can have her start using the party as a cat's paw to do heroic things and buck the system in ways she feels she can't.<ul><li>The Immaculate Faith would move against her because of their core beliefs, the Sidereals for similar and have already proven they're willing to be horrible when they murdered the Solars, and then greedy/power-hungry scions of her own Realm houses wouldn't want the status quo changed.</li> <li>You can have her feel trapped by the system that she created, but also paralyzed by her fear over how horrible things were during the Contagion and Balorian Crusade as that was so horrific that I imagine they'd give ptsd.</li> <li>Up to you if this is openly or not, there are benefits to both. I lean toward in disguise because it can create drama later with a big reveal and because players are bad about accidentally sharing info they shouldn't. I can easily believe that the Empress would be able to recognize that one of the player characters is a liability in that regard.</li> <li>This story works best for solars, dragon-blooded, or lunars in my estimation. If you don't want that to happen, or are running a different faction, you can have her be an odd advisor to Roseblack (see below).</li> </ul></li> <li>When she sees that the world is at a tipping point, she disappears, just like in the core timeline.<ul><li>She knows that she's going to be a target by the deathlords and every other faction including some of the nascent solars that she would like to ally with.</li> <li>If she helps fight against these threats, and wins, then she'll be even more of linchpin then before, which means that the world is even more dependent on her and a system that she feels trapped by.</li> <li>She knows that if things do go super wrong, that she has the Imperial Manse in her back pocket.</li> </ul></li> <li>Scarlet Empress then goes to advise the PCs and/or the Roseblack on things as the classic mysterious mentor.<ul><li>This will make the players feel special. Players love when cool npcs say that their characters are cool.</li> <li>She can serve as a way for the storyteller to give information that there's really not a good way for the players to know it.<ul><li>An easy way to do this is, instead of the answer, the Empress gives information that allows the players to find the answer, such as a lost manse, or a secret unguarded passage that leads to the Underworld. This includes even information on how to possibly break the Great Curse. She probably doesn't know about that, but has suspicions like you see a few sidereals have, and then that allows the players an in-character path to trying to fix the world.</li> <li>Why not give the information or fight the fights? Besides the fact that it might overshadow the PCs, especially if they're dragon-blooded, is that she's trying to not be a linchpin anymore. Flavor-wise, the helps reconnect all the strands of fate that are bound into her back into the world.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul>What are people's thoughts?\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25211-justinfernal","name":"justinfernal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25211_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-19T23:00:47-07:0